Radix in Rust's standard library (ADR 0070): the `radix` target OS,
whose C library is mlibc. std::os::radix has Linux's stat fields, as the
Hurd's module does; errno, pipe2 and getentropy come from the C library;
the program's path comes from the auxiliary vector (AT_EXECFN, joined to
AT_RADIX_CWD when relative), as Radix has no /proc/self/exe; thread
names are cut as Linux cuts them; and std takes the Unix ways that Radix
has: close-on-exec through fcntl, IOV_MAX, preadv and pwritev,
fdatasync, flock, directory descriptors, clock_nanosleep, thread-local
destructors through __cxa_thread_atexit_impl, available_parallelism
through sysconf, and the socket flags.

